{"kind":"request","text":"Please review J1 again at the exact head, under request 7d6ce0f6 and promise 3649cf4a. This replaces head 00fed3ed, after your verdict 384a99aa.\n\nThe gate passed at the checkpoint in the note, and the head adds only the implementation note and its manifest. The witness and the last controls ran at c50c9723, and the next commit changes one line of the design note. This is hosted code. The planner deploys it later.\n\nYour five items, and what I did:\n1. keep refuses a kind over 256 bytes. The comment in originals.ts states why 64 rows fit the list's reply. openOriginals now tells a failed read from an empty list: the page says so and keeps the last rows, the lock and the button.\n2. At the object's cap, keep first drops rows of members no longer active or with no active key, in a bounded quantum, then answers room-full, with its own status 423 and its own words. A retired key whose member has another active key keeps its rows, as section 8 requires.\n3. keepToSend runs the check before the keep, and anything that stops the send after the keep ends the row. If that DELETE also fails, the words say the row stays. The retained editor runs its check again after the keep.\n4. A page that held a session and failed to renew it stops the send and says why. A page that opened on signed reads sends as before.\n5. The comments in contract session.ts, client session.ts, sessions.ts and originals.ts state what a session can make. Section 9 corrects the reason for connection requests and states the limits on issueInvitation and issueDeviceKey. From the code, issueInvitation skips the copy and keeps its own browser record. issueDeviceKey makes a row, so its button works, and it skips the lock and the fence.\n\nFrom your list that did not block, the button now lifts its own page's lastActs and unknown fence, and the next send clears not-kept. Section 9 names the stale device link and the 404 against 403 difference.\n\nPlease test these points:\n1. My decision on item 2. The object's cap does not refuse a request to this membership scope, so an admin can still remove a member. A room's total can then pass 1 GiB, bounded only by each member's 16 MiB, and nobody measured that against the object's storage limit. I accepted that because it takes many admitted members filling their caps on purpose. Is the bound good enough, or do you want a second, higher cap on those rows?\n2. A member with no active key loses rows on a full object. Is dropping them right, given that a recovery could otherwise settle them?\n3. 14 of the earlier controls did not rerun. controls/not-rerun.txt shows each break's line still occurs once, and their cases now read rows through a test helper. Is that enough, or should they run again?\n4. The reload limit. A reload during a failed renewal loses the memory of a held session, so that page opens on signed reads and sends with no copy.\n\nNo deployment is requested with this head. The planner's deployment after this landing also opens the hosted endpoint on dap-demo, as asked in 456f5a3e. That is the planner's decision and is not part of this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4353e21bd4b64cdd0e63433809939d4094cc273d","branch":"builder/j1-design-20261031","conditions":"Independent exact-head review of f63f0015…02a6e14 with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming git:sha1…4cc273d first and resting on all 36 artifacts.","head":"f63f0015fa51b900f46ef554aa296a99a02a6e14","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"4817b532e7a1a118634cb7947b05f7d5d16f726c9d30ea22ff7eaf7db3634e80"}}